WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.902
Direct prompt injection happens when a user directly enters instructions intended to override or manipulate the application's behavior.

2
00:00:07.902 --> 00:00:10.806
The input comes from the user interaction itself.

3
00:00:10.806 --> 00:00:25.104
Indirect prompt injection happens when malicious or manipulative instructions are hidden in content the model later processes, such as a web page, email, ticket, document, repository file, or retrieved knowledge base entry.

4
00:00:25.104 --> 00:00:31.500
Indirect injection is especially dangerous because the person using the AI system may not see the instruction.

5
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:40.879
The model may treat untrusted external content as valid guidance unless the surrounding application clearly limits how that content can influence behavior.

6
00:00:40.879 --> 00:00:51.425
The risk becomes more serious when the LLM can call tools, read sensitive data, send messages, update tickets, inspect repositories, or modify systems.

7
00:00:51.425 --> 00:00:58.319
The issue is not only whether the model can be influenced, but what capabilities are reachable after influence occurs.
